Day#16 - Gesture Recognition in XR

Introduction--
--Gesture recognition in extended reality (XR) replaces physical controllers with natural hand tracking, using built-in sensors to detect finger shapes,
wrist rotations, and spatial movements.

Core Concepts-
Core ConceptsStatic Poses:
--Fixed hand shapes combined with orientation constraints (e.g., a thumbs-up or a Vulcan salute).
--Dynamic Gestures: Actions involving motion, such as swiping left or right, or micro-gestures performed with subtle thumb movements.
--Skeletal Tracking: Mapping precise 3D joints and coordinates of the human hand via infrared or RGB cameras.

Advantages--
--Natural movement: Users interact with virtual elements using normal hand motions, mimicking real life.
--Low learning curve: People adapt quickly because the gestures rely on everyday actions like pointing or pinching.
--Better social presence: Virtual avatars can accurately mirror real non-verbal communication and expressions

Types of Gesture in XR--
--Static Gestures (Poses): Formed by holding a specific hand shape and orientation (such as a thumbs-up, pointing, or stop sign) without rapid movement.
--Dynamic Gestures (Motions): Involve movement over time, such as swiping, waving to open a menu, or throwing a virtual object.

How it works--
How It Works
--Cameras and Sensors: Headset cameras track your hand and finger positions in real time.
--Static Gestures: Tapping or pinching fingers together acts like a mouse click.Dynamic Gestures: Moving or swiping your hand side-to-side scrolls through pages or menus.
